Rationale: The claim states that the Inglis Barracks bombing was the first IRA attack in England since a bombing whose purpose was to kill Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher. While the evidence identifies the 1996 Docklands bombing as the last IRA attack in England before the Inglis Barracks bombing, it does not confirm that the 1996 Docklands bombing had the purpose of killing Margaret Thatcher. The Brighton hotel bombing of 1984, which did aim to kill Thatcher, is not mentioned as the last IRA attack in England before the Inglis Barracks bombing. Therefore, the evidence is insufficient to confirm the material part of the claim regarding the purpose of the prior attack.
